# packaging

## docker
- label: GitHub Actions publishes the Docker image for both linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 platforms so Apple Silicon users can run ghcr.io/av/pace:latest
  command: grep -q 'platforms: linux/amd64,linux/arm64' .github/workflows/docker.yml
  tags: [spec, implemented]

# domain
- a Dashboard is the self-hosted web interface that displays aggregated, deduplicated, and optionally LLM-processed content from many sources in a single customizable view
- a Source is a content provider (Hacker News, Reddit, arXiv, RSS feeds, GitHub releases, podcasts, etc.) that a user configures to pull Items into the Dashboard
- an Item is a single piece of content (story, paper, video, release note, etc.) shown in the Dashboard, with title, link, publication time, and optional summary or body
- a Panel is a section of the Dashboard that shows a curated list of Items from one or more Sources after the user's configured Transforms have been applied
- a Transform is a processing step (deduplicate, keyword filter/exclude, keep latest N, LLM summarize/rank/filter, time decay scoring, etc.) that the user declares in their Config to control which Items appear and in what order
- a Config is the single YAML file in which the user declares adapters[] (optional refresh_interval per ingest entry on IngestAdapterConfig; Adapter.fetch contract is type+params only), optional pipelines, Transforms, the recursive flexbox Layout of Panels and Widgets (panel source objects only adapter and params), and optional LLM provider settings
- a Preset is a ready-to-use Config file (tech-news, ml-ai, academic-papers, product-launches, etc.) that can be selected with --preset or -P so a user can get a working Dashboard instantly without writing YAML
- a Refresh updates the Items for a Panel (or all Panels) by re-fetching from the underlying Sources, re-applying Transforms, and redrawing the Dashboard; users can trigger it manually via a button or it happens on the schedule defined per adapters[] entry (and per pipeline when configured), not on panel layout source objects
- LLM-powered Transforms (summarize, filter, rank by interests) are optional; when no LLM is configured or the provider is unreachable, the Dashboard still works normally using only the non-LLM Transforms
- the entire system runs as a single Docker container (or local Bun process) serving server-rendered HTML with no client-side JavaScript; the Dashboard is responsive and works on phones, tablets, and desktops
- a Widget is a layout node that displays static content (an image, text/markdown, or embedded iframe) rather than adapter-sourced Items; widgets are not Panels and are skipped by collectPanels @implemented
- a Share Storage Backend is a storage target such as GitHub Gist that saves Static Dashboard artifacts and returns an artifact identifier or raw artifact URL @implemented
- a Static Dashboard is a read-only snapshot of a Dashboard rendered to portable files that can be opened without running a pace server @implemented
- a Share Renderer is a service such as gisthost.github.io or gistpreview.github.io that turns stored Static Dashboard artifacts into a browser-rendered share URL @implemented

# content

## dedup
- Items from different Sources that point to the same story are automatically deduplicated so a user does not see the same link multiple times in one Panel

## filtering
- Users can add keyword filters or excludes in their Config so that, for example, hiring posts or off-topic content never appear in the Dashboard

# usage

## refresh
- Clicking the refresh button on a Panel immediately fetches the latest Items from its Sources and updates the view without restarting the server

## startup
- Running 'pace' or 'pace serve' (or the Docker image) starts a web server on port 7453 by default and begins populating the Dashboard from the configured Sources within seconds

## onboarding
- The Dashboard works with zero configuration using the built-in example (or any Preset) and requires no database setup beyond the automatically created data/ directory
- A user can go from zero to a useful personalized tech/news/research Dashboard in under a minute by running the Docker image with --preset tech-news or ml-ai

# llm

## user-experience
- When an LLM is configured, Items can show AI-written summaries and be sorted according to the user's stated interests; without an LLM the same Transforms and layout still work

# cli

## usage
- The CLI accepts --config to point at a custom YAML file and --port to change the listening port; --help shows all options

# reliability
- If a Source is temporarily unreachable, the Dashboard continues to show the last successfully fetched Items for that Source instead of going blank or crashing
- label: When refresh health is degraded, the Dashboard shows an error notice naming failing Sources while continuing to render their cached Items; a later successful refresh removes the automatic notice
  command: bun test src/server.test.ts --test-name-pattern 'shows degraded source notice with cached content and clears it after recovery'
  tags: [spec, implemented, cached-failure-notice]
- label: Shared adapter HTTP errors redact sensitive URL query values before reaching scheduler logs, health responses, or refresh error bodies, while preserving the URL host, path, query key names, nonsensitive query values, and HTTP status for diagnosis
  command: bun test src/fetch.test.ts --test-name-pattern 'redacts sensitive query credentials from fetch errors'
  tags: [spec, implemented, url-secret-redaction]
- label: URL diagnostics never expose embedded basic-auth userinfo: safe URL config fields reject username/password without echoing them, and shared adapter fetch errors replace runtime userinfo while preserving scheme, host, path, safe query detail, and status
  command: bun test src/url-credential-diagnostics.test.ts
  tags: [spec, implemented, url-userinfo-redaction]

# code-quality
- transforms.ts ContentItemRow handling uses only the current panel_id field name (post DB rename from adapter_name column); the two obsolete .adapter_name references (in contentItemToRow and cluster signal extraction) have been removed so rows always conform to the ContentItemRow interface and source fallback uses panel_id when applicable (verifiable by: ! grep -q "adapter_name" src/transforms.ts ) @implemented
- config.ts validateAdapterConfig reuses validateOptionalNonEmptyString for the optional adapter.name field (no inline duplication of the typeof+trim non-empty check logic that the helper already provides and which is used for similar optional string fields like half_life/criteria/prompt); verifiable by: grep -q "validateOptionalNonEmptyString(adapter.name" src/config.ts @implemented
- core modules cli.ts/config.ts/index.ts/scheduler.ts no longer import shared helpers (errorMessage, parsePort, isValidPort, getAdapterName) from ./adapters/types.ts violating layer boundaries (Convention Blindness); instead they import from canonical src/utils.ts, and src/adapters/types.ts reexports only for its internal/adapter compat (verifiable by: grep -q 'from ["\./]utils' src/cli.ts && grep -q 'from ["\./]utils' src/config.ts && grep -q 'from ["\./]utils' src/index.ts && grep -q 'from ["\./]utils' src/scheduler.ts && ! grep -E 'from ["\./]*adapters/types' src/{cli,config,index,scheduler}.ts | grep -E '(errorMessage|parsePort|isValidPort|getAdapterName)' && grep -q 'from "../utils"' src/adapters/types.ts ) @implemented
- scheduler.ts refresh/prune failure console.warn uses "failed to ..." phrasing (db/index/cli parity), not the old em-dash error pattern; executeWithRunningGuard and pruneOldItems use errorMessage(err); prune keeps warn+continue with no rethrow (verifiable by: grep -q "scheduler: failed to refresh" src/scheduler.ts && grep -q "scheduler: failed to prune:" src/scheduler.ts && grep -q "errorMessage(err)" src/scheduler.ts ) @implemented
- src/db.ts getDb() and closeDb() no longer contain bare 'catch {}' silent swallows on db.close(); instead they use errorMessage(err) in a console.warn with "db: failed to close:" (for debuggability and convention consistency with scheduler/b5h and db error prefixes) while still swallowing to preserve original shutdown/switch behavior; thrown db errors use "db: failed to …" phrasing for open/init/save/replace (verifiable by: grep -q 'db: failed to close:' src/db.ts && grep -q 'db: failed to open' src/db.ts && grep -q 'db: failed to replace' src/db.ts && grep -q 'errorMessage(err)' src/db.ts && ! grep -E 'catch\s*\{\s*\}' src/db.ts ) @implemented
- src/index.ts contains no over-engineered isAllAdapter helper function (nor its formal JSDoc claiming "Single source of truth for the special \"all\" adapter sentinel"); the predicate is inlined at its three call sites using the direct `=== "all"` check for improved readability with no behavior change (verifiable by: ! grep -q "isAllAdapter" src/index.ts && ! grep -q "Single source of truth for the special" src/index.ts ) @implemented
- src/layout.tsx contains no over-verbose defaultFlex tiny wrapper helper function (nor its call from flexStyle); default flex logic (f ?? 1) is inlined directly inside flexStyle; flexStyle has no restating JSDoc comment block claiming to be a 'Shared helper to avoid repeating' the flex pattern (verifiable by: ! grep -q 'function defaultFlex' src/layout.tsx && ! grep -q 'Shared helper to avoid repeating' src/layout.tsx && grep -q 'flex:${f ?? 1}' src/layout.tsx ) @implemented
- src/cli.ts contains no verbosity-inflating restating comments for the projectRoot chdir bootstrap (the 3-line 'Ensure we always run...' block) or the unknown-options rejection (the 2-line 'Reject unknown options...' block); both have been removed so code stands alone without duplication of its own intent (verifiable by: ! grep -q 'Ensure we always run from the project root' src/cli.ts && ! grep -q 'Reject unknown options' src/cli.ts ) @implemented
- src/llm.ts contains no over-engineered extractText tiny private helper function (nor its JSDoc "Extract text from a pi-ai AssistantMessage response"); the 1-line extraction logic is inlined directly at its single callsite inside safeComplete for improved readability with no behavior change (verifiable by: ! grep -q "function extractText" src/llm.ts && ! grep -q "Extract text from a pi-ai" src/llm.ts && grep -q 'type: "text"; text: string' src/llm.ts ) @implemented
- src/dedupe.ts normalizeUrl reuses a single computation of the (possibly empty) search string instead of calling params.toString() twice in the conditional expression for parsed.search (verifiable by: ! grep -F 'params.toString() ? `?${params.toString()}`' src/dedupe.ts ) @implemented
